This is the standard fannkuch-redux benchmark. The output is the checksum, then `Pfannkuchen(N) = max`.

The approach is to split the work by the first element of the permutation and run the pieces in parallel. A faster variant would fix the first two elements, but fixing one is enough here.

For each first-element value `f` in 0..N-1, I build the starting permutation using the factorial-number-system indexing from the standard solution. The standard approach splits the permutations into chunks by index range. Each chunk starts from its own permutation index, which is computed from the factorial decomposition, and then advances with the standard next-permutation rotation step.

Per-chunk procedure:
- `fact[i]` holds the factorials.
- With `taskSize = fact[N] / chunks`, chunk `t` begins at index `idx = t*taskSize`.
- Build the starting permutation `p` from `idx`:
  - `p = [0..N-1]`
  - `for i = N-1 down to 1`: `d = idx / fact[i]`, `idx %= fact[i]`, `count[i] = d`, then rotate `p[0..i]` left by `d` using a temp array.
- Loop over the chunk's `taskSize` permutations:
  - If `p[0] != 0`, copy `p` to `q` and count flips: `while (f = q[0]) != 0`, reverse `q[0..f]`, and increment the flip count.
  - Update the max, and add or subtract the flips to the checksum depending on the parity of the index.
  - Advance to the next permutation: swap `p[0]` and `p[1]`, then `i = 1` and `count[i]++`. While `count[i] > i`, set `count[i] = 0`, then `i++`, rotate `p[0..i]` left by one, and increment `count[i]`. Stop if `i` reaches N.

Rotation in the next-permutation step: after the first swap, loop `i=1`; `while count[i] >= i+1`, set `count[i]=0`, `i++`, `t=p[0]`, shift `p[0..i-1]=p[1..i]`, `p[i]=t`, and increment `count[i]`. That is the standard form:

```
first = p[0]; p[0]=p[1]; p[1]=first;
i=1; while(++count[i] > i){ count[i]=0; i++; p[0]=p[1]; ... }
```

The reference version is:

```
t=p[1]; p[1]=p[0]; p[0]=t;
i=1;
while(++count[i] > i) { count[i++]=0; t=p[0]; for j<i+1: p[j]=p[j+1]; p[i]=t; }
```

Here, `p[0]` is the value after the swap, and the rotation is over `p[0..i]`. With the swap done first and the rotation performed at the next level `i`, this is the known-correct form. At the last permutation, `i` can reach N, so I guard with `if (i>=N) break` and size `count` as N+1.

The starting-permutation construction must be consistent with this enumeration. I use the same rotation: for `i = N-1 down to 1`, `d = idx / fact[i]`, then rotate `p[0..i]` left by `d`. This matches the reference implementation.

